   "input": "/bin/zsh -c 'cat /var/folders/3g/vf36b0b14wzd0fgsgtdp06h00000gp/T/writing-probe/p-985c79b7/48acec06.txt'",
   "output": "You are comparing two drafts written from the same brief. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; judge only the text below.\n\nBrief: Write the first two sections, about 350 words, of a lesson for engineers titled 'Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory'.\n\n<<<DRAFT A\n# Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory\n\n## Try a rename with a saved link\n\nKestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms, each with a room key and a human-readable display name. Room owners can change the display name. When a room needs a different label, a rename lets the owner change what people see.\n\nStart by checking that a saved room link still works after the change:\n\n1. Save a link that identifies the room by its room key.\n2. Rename the room.\n3. Open the saved link.\n4. Confirm that it reaches the same room and shows the new display name.\n\nFor an illustrative example, an owner could rename a room from \u201cPlanning\u201d to \u201cProject planning.\u201d Save its room link before making that change. When you open the saved link afterward, the expected result is the original room with \u201cProject planning\u201d displayed.\n\nCheck both parts of that result. Reaching the same room verifies that the saved link still identifies it. Seeing the new name verifies that Kestrel displays the changed label when the link opens.\n\n## Understand what the link identifies\n\nA room key is assigned when the room is created. It never changes, and Kestrel never reuses it for another room. This gives the room an identity that remains the same through a rename.\n\nThe display name is the label people see. Because owners can change that label, it can differ between two visits to the same room. A new name does not change the room key.\n\nWhen someone opens a saved room link, Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room\u2019s current name. The link therefore continues to identify the room even when its visible label changes.\n\nA link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename. Its name has changed, so the name used to build that link may no longer find it. Use the room key when building a room link.\n\nIn the storage schema, `room_ref` is the key column and `title_text` is the display-name column. When working with these columns, keep their roles clear: `room_ref` holds the room\u2019s permanent key, while `title_text` holds the label an owner can change.\nDRAFT A>>>\n\n<<<DRAFT B\n# Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory\n\n## 1. Identify a room by its key\n\nKestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms. Each room has a room key and a display name. Before renaming a room, understand which value identifies it and which value people see.\n\nThe room key is assigned when the room is created. It never changes, and it is never reused for another room. A rename therefore leaves the room\u2019s key unchanged: the room has a new label, but the key still identifies the same room.\n\nThe display name is the human-readable label shown to people. Room owners can change it. When you work with the storage schema, these concepts have different column names: `room_ref` holds the room key, and `title_text` holds the display name.\n\nKeep that distinction clear when working with links. A saved room link identifies its destination by the room key. When someone opens it, Kestrel looks up that key and displays the room\u2019s current name. The name shown when the link opens can therefore differ from the name the room had when the link was saved.\n\n## 2. Verify a rename with a saved link\n\nTo verify a rename, start before changing the display name. Save a link to the room, then rename the room and open that saved link.\n\nUse this sequence:\n\n1. Save a room link before the rename.\n2. Change the room\u2019s display name.\n3. Open the link you saved.\n4. Check that it reaches the same room and shows the new name.\n\nBoth parts of the final check matter. Reaching the same room verifies that the saved link still identifies its destination after the rename. Seeing the new name verifies that Kestrel displays the room\u2019s current label when the link opens.\n\nA link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename because the name has changed. For this verification, use the saved room link that identifies the room by its key.\n\nThe expected result is straightforward: the link saved before the rename reaches the same room afterward, and Kestrel shows the new display name. The key remains unchanged throughout.\nDRAFT B>>>\n\nAnswer each question with \"A\", \"B\", or \"SAME\":\n- \"reader\": Could a first-time reader do what they came to do?\n- \"careful\": Does it read like a careful person wrote it?\n- \"overall\": Which draft is better overall?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"reader\": \"A\", \"careful\": \"A\", \"overall\": \"A\", \"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"}",
